e entry's declaration everywhere (`toolbelt.coerce`): a ref may be `file::name` (did-you-mean on a miss), a string `@path`, a list `a,b`; an undeclared or missing param refuses by name. Entries are plain Lua files in `lua/cartograph/tactics/` — no central list; kinds `write` (build(params) -> a tactic term) and `discovery` (measure + claim, `measures` = the ticket it tracks); each carries `examples` the fence runs (tests/toolbelt_spec.lua). Today: align-family (write), learn-from-example / promote-tactic (write), family-premise, propagate-reach [CART-1155], witness-shape-collision [CART-1154], spec-fails, mutation-check [CART-1174] |\n| Does this spec actually CATCH a regression in this guard? (a mutation check — never sed the working tree) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua run mutation-check - file=<rel> before='<expr or chunk>' after='<mutated>' spec=<name>_spec [repo=<dir>] [keep=1]` — copies the repo (all but .git) to a scratch root, runs the spec there FIRST (a red baseline or a run of nothing REFUSES), applies the mutation by example (rewrite-by-example: staged, parse-guarded; a mutation that matches nothing REFUSES by name), runs the spec again: claim holds = CAUGHT, fails = SURVIVED (both summaries in the why). An expression is read as `return <expr>` when both sides are expressions (a leading `#` would otherwise read as a shebang, CART-1177). The rule generalizes carried operands — the claim says how many sites it mutated. `ground=1` applies EXACTLY the written text at its one site through the edit verb's classification (no rule is learned; a result that already occurs elsewhere refuses as drifted — widen the anchor): use it for any mutation inside a boolean chain or a comparison, where a learned rule mutates many unrelated sites and a SURVIVED describes a different mutant (MEASURED: an `or`-chain edit in xlang.lua learned as 4 rules at 10 sites SURVIVED; ground, it was CAUGHT). The working tree is never written. `spec-fails` (discovery) is the oracle alone: `run spec-fails - spec=<x>_spec [root=…]`. A mutant that makes the spec HANG (an infinite loop) is a FAILURE named `TIMED OUT` — the runner is its own process group and is killed whole at the timeout (before 2026-09-30 the tactic raised, and each hung `node` child spun on at 100% CPU). CART-1174 |\n| How much of these commits did CARTOGRAPH write (vs hand edits)? (the in-the-loop ratio) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua run provenance-audit - [range=HEAD~20..HEAD] [repo=<dir>]` — every journal whose recorded root lies in the repo (journal.survey), each entry's ADDED lines matched as a multiset against each commit's `git show -U0` added lines (line level: a mixed commit splits honestly); per commit explained vs hand bytes, the overall ratio (reported, never a gate). Baseline 2026-09-28: 0.13% of the last 30 commits. Not yet: stale decisions / placeholders (the ledger, CART-1180). CART-1181 |\n| Which journal entries (and which DECISIONS) wrote this commit? Does the provenance travel with the repo? (the ledger) | Every journal entry records `decided_by` (`tactic` / `direct`) and `decisions = { { kind, key, by = accept-list \\| remembered, decision_id } }` (CART-1179). The LEDGER: one git note per commit on `refs/notes/cartograph` — `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/provledger.lua write [<rev>]` / `show [<rev>]`; `.githooks/post-commit` writes it after every commit (never fails a commit). In Lua: `require('cartograph.provenance').attribute(repo, range)` (the shared line-level attribution), `.ledger_row`, `.write_note`, `.read_note`. Notes travel only when pushed (`git push origin refs/notes/cartograph`). ★ The ledger only TIGHTENS: a note claiming a decision accepted answers nothing (pinned). An entry explains ONLY the commit its change landed in (CART-1195): made no later than the commit, not already in its parent, and — when older than the parent commit — none of it in the parent; before, 22 of 135 entries were credited to several commits. CART-1180 |\n| May this record LEAVE THE MACHINE? (a ledger note, a token, an invocation — does it carry an untracked file's bytes, a `.env`?) | `require('cartograph.sensitive')` — `classify(root, rels)` → `{ [rel] = why }`: DERIVED, a file git tracks is public, untracked/ignored is sensitive, a world with no repo is all sensitive; user `config.at(<abs>, 'sensitive') = true/false` overrides (never the tree). `reference(v)` = what travels in a sensitive value's place (`{ ref = 'sha256:…', sensitive = true }`). `leaks(record, images)` = the EGRESS check, independent of the redaction: any line (≥ 12 chars) of a sensitive file's images anywhere in the record's strings → `{ file, why }`, the bytes never named. Wired: every journal entry now records its INVOCATION (`{ verb, args, where }`, CART-1191 leaf 1 — local); the ledger note carries each entry's invocation with paths named `@<world id>/…`, or — when the entry touched a sensitive file — only `{ verb, args = reference, sensitive_files = n }`; `write_note` refuses a note `leaks` flags (class `decision`, nothing written). CART-1192 / CART-1193 |\n| Merge ANOTHER checkout's work into this one — without a line or syntax merge (their intents, replayed here) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/replay.lua <into dir> <from repo> <range> [apply=1] [approvals=<dir>]`, or OVER A SHARED REMOTE `tools/replay.lua <into dir> --remote <name> <range>` (the teammate pushed their branch AND `refs/notes/cartograph`; `replay.fetch` brings the notes into a MIRROR, `refs/notes/cartograph-remotes/<name>`, never merged into this repo's own; no git merge happens — their intents are replayed in your working tree) — reads the INVOCATIONS the ledger notes of `<range>` carry (journal entries record `{ verb, args }` since CART-1191 leaf 1) and re-runs them here in order through the tactic runner; each step classifies ITSELF by its verb's goal check: `done` (already here — the same change on both sides), `applied`, `conflict` (the verb refused