gate-reach [CART-1155], witness-shape-collision [CART-1154], spec-fails, mutation-check [CART-1174] |\n| Does this spec actually CATCH a regression in this guard? (a mutation check — never sed the working tree) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua run mutation-check - file=<rel> before='<expr or chunk>' after='<mutated>' spec=<name>_spec [repo=<dir>] [keep=1]` — copies the repo (all but .git) to a scratch root, runs the spec there FIRST (a red baseline or a run of nothing REFUSES), applies the mutation by example (rewrite-by-example: staged, parse-guarded; a mutation that matches nothing REFUSES by name), runs the spec again: claim holds = CAUGHT, fails = SURVIVED (both summaries in the why). An expression is read as `return <expr>` when both sides are expressions (a leading `#` would otherwise read as a shebang, CART-1177). The rule generalizes carried operands — the claim says how many sites it mutated. `ground=1` applies EXACTLY the written text at its one site through the edit verb's classification (no rule is learned; a result that already occurs elsewhere refuses as drifted — widen the anchor): use it for any mutation inside a boolean chain or a comparison, where a learned rule mutates many unrelated sites and a SURVIVED describes a different mutant (MEASURED: an `or`-chain edit in xlang.lua learned as 4 rules at 10 sites SURVIVED; ground, it was CAUGHT). The working tree is never written. `spec-fails` (discovery) is the oracle alone: `run spec-fails - spec=<x>_spec [root=…]`. A mutant that makes the spec HANG (an infinite loop) is a FAILURE named `TIMED OUT` — the runner is its own process group and is killed whole at the timeout (before 2026-09-30 the tactic raised, and each hung `node` child spun on at 100% CPU). CART-1174 |\n| How much of these commits did CARTOGRAPH write (vs hand edits)? (the in-the-loop ratio) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua run provenance-audit - [range=HEAD~20..HEAD] [repo=<dir>]` — every journal whose rec